{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It requires comparing the outcomes of the two lotteries and assessing whether their differences are considered negligible, then preferring the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. In this case, Lottery A offers 5 Euros with 100% probability, while Lottery B offers 5000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. The outcomes differ substantially in amount (5 vs. 5000 Euros), but the decision reason assumes these differences are negligible. If that assumption holds, the 'favorable outcome' could be interpreted as any positive outcome. Under this interpretation, Lottery A has a 100% chance of a positive outcome, while Lottery B has only a 0.1% chance. Thus, the reason would prefer Lottery A. Alternatively, if the 'favorable outcome' is defined as the high outcome (5000 Euros), then only Lottery B offers it, but with very low probability. However, the reason explicitly states that outcome differences are negligible, so the focus should be on the likelihood of any positive outcome, favoring Lottery A.",
